  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1215 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe long term will largely see the large scale pattern featuring\na broad 500mb ridge gradually shifting from the western CONUS \nto the eastern CONUS, keeping our area under the influence of\nupper ridging through the period. The two exceptions are the\nremnants of tropical system Isaias, currently in the Gulf of\nAmerica, moves east of the area this weekend, then an uncertain\npotential for a pattern changes toward the middle of next week.\n\nHighs will be in the 80s to low 90s through at least Monday,\nthen potentially a few degrees cooler Tuesday and Wednesday, but\nwith high model spread leading to low confidence next week.\nDaily RH values through at least the weekend are forecast to \ngenerally be 20-30% (potentially lower at times) along and E of \nHwy 65 and 30-40% west of Hwy 65. Winds will be light (generally\nless than 15 mph), keeping fire weather threats low. Stronger\nwinds with gusts up to 20-30 mph are possible early to middle of\nnext week, but there is quite a bit of uncertainty in wind\nspeeds, temperatures, and humidity levels.\n\nWeekend rainfall potential depends on the track of the tropical\nsystem. Expect a sharp cutoff on the western side due to ridging\nand dry air in place. NBM probabilities for >=0.1\" of rainfall\nlate Saturday into early Sunday are generally 20-40% along/east\nof a line from Gainesville, MO to Rolla, MO.",
